---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CtaBand from '../../components/CtaBand.astro';
import IndustryPageTemplate from '../../components/IndustryPageTemplate.astro';

const pageTitle = 'Sight Glass for Steam & Boiler Systems';
const metaTitle = 'Sight Glass for Steam & Boiler Systems | High Temperature Sight Glass — SIGHTSPEC';
const kw = 'sight glass for steam and boiler systems';
const desc = 'High temperature sight glasses for steam and boiler systems: SG-400 flanged and SG-300 threaded models in carbon steel with borosilicate or quartz windows for boiler drums, steam mains, condensate systems and heat exchangers.';
const points = [
  'Borosilicate glass to DIN 7080 rated to 572°F (300°C), with quartz windows available for superheated steam and higher temperatures',
  'Carbon steel bodies with graphite-reinforced gaskets built for high-temperature steam and boiler duty',
  'SG-400 flanged per ANSI Class 150/300 or DIN PN10–PN40 for boiler drums, steam mains and heat exchanger observation',
  'SG-300 threaded rated to 100 bar (1450 psi) for instrument lines, steam tracing and small-bore viewing',
];
const longtail = [
  'high temperature sight glass',
  'steam boiler sight glass',
  'quartz sight glass',
  'sight glass for steam lines',
  'boiler drum sight glass',
  'carbon steel sight glass steam',
  'sight glass thermal shock',
  'boiler water level sight glass',
  'sight glass for condensate systems',
  'heat exchanger sight glass',
];
const recommended = [
  { href: '/products/flanged-sight-glasses/', title: 'SG-400 Flanged Sight Glass', desc: 'ANSI Class 150/300 flanged, 2"–8", carbon steel bodies, quartz window option for superheated steam.' },
  { href: '/products/threaded-sight-glasses/', title: 'SG-300 Threaded Sight Glass', desc: 'Male NPT/BSP 1/4"–3", up to 100 bar (1450 psi), compact for instrument lines and steam tracing.' },
  { href: '/products/sight-flow-indicators/', title: 'SG-200 Sight Flow Indicator', desc: 'Flapper, rotary and drip-tube types for condensate return and steam trap discharge viewing.' },
];
const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is the maximum temperature for a sight glass in steam service?', a: 'The borosilicate window is rated to 572°F (300°C). Where steam temperatures run beyond that — superheated steam, for example — we supply quartz windows on the SG-400 flanged sight glass. Always state both operating and upset temperature so the glass, gasket and body are matched to the duty.' },
  { q: 'How do I protect a sight glass from thermal shock?', a: 'Thermal shock is the most common cause of glass breakage in steam service — a cold glass hit by hot steam, or cold condensate splashing a hot glass. Mitigate it with a proper warm-up: drain condensate, pressurize slowly, and let the glass heat gradually before full steam flow. For frequent cycling or higher temperatures, choose the quartz window option, and fit a protective shield where impact or debris is a risk.' },
  { q: 'Is the sight glass compliant with boiler codes?', a: 'The glass is manufactured to DIN 7080, a recognized standard for pressure sight glass, and pressure equipment is supplied to PED where applicable. Final boiler-code compliance depends on the vessel design, operating conditions and your jurisdiction — send your drawings and we will provide the material documentation your inspector needs.' },
  { q: 'Quartz or borosilicate — which glass for steam?', a: 'Borosilicate to DIN 7080 is the standard choice and covers most steam duty to 572°F (300°C). Quartz extends the temperature range beyond that and tolerates thermal cycling better, at a higher cost. For superheated steam, rapid temperature swings, or maximum shock resistance, specify the quartz option.' },
  { q: 'Can I use a sight glass to check boiler water level?', a: 'Yes — sight glasses on boiler drums and steam generators give operators direct visual confirmation of water level. Keep the window clean and inspect it regularly; scale from hard feed water can obscure the view over time. Code-governed level indication may require dedicated instrumentation — confirm the requirement with your boiler inspector.' },
  { q: 'Can I see condensate flow and steam trap operation?', a: 'Yes. The SG-200 sight flow indicator with flapper, rotary or drip-tube internals confirms condensate flow, trap discharge and valve position at a glance, and the threaded version suits small condensate and instrument lines.' },
];
const blogLinks = [
  { href: '/blog/sight-glass-glass-types/', title: 'Sight Glass Glass Types: Borosilicate vs Quartz vs Soda Lime' },
];
---

<Layout title={metaTitle} description={desc}>
  <IndustryPageTemplate title={pageTitle} kw={kw} desc={desc} points={points} longtail={longtail} recommended={recommended} faqs={faqs} blogLinks={blogLinks}>
    <div slot="chapters">
      <section class="bg-white">
        <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-blue">STEAM DUTY</p>
          <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy-950 sm:text-3xl">Where Sight Glasses Sit in Steam and Boiler Systems</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Steam systems put sight glasses at high temperature, and often at pressure: steam lines, boiler drums, steam generators, condensate systems and heat exchangers. The observation duty changes with location — water level on the drum, flow and phase on the lines, condensate quality on the return — but every location shares the same two constraints: temperature that exceeds what ordinary glass handles, and pressure that the whole assembly must contain.</p>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600"><strong class="text-navy-950">Glass temperature limit.</strong> The practical ceiling for the standard borosilicate window is 572°F (300°C) — a limit set by the glass, not the metal. Below that, borosilicate to DIN 7080 is the workhorse: low thermal expansion and good resistance to clean steam and hot condensate. Above it — superheated steam, high-pressure boiler duty — the window is switched to quartz, which carries higher temperature and tolerates sharper thermal gradients. On the SG-400 flanged sight glass, both options fit the same body.</p>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600"><strong class="text-navy-950">Pressure considerations.</strong> Steam-side pressure is carried by the assembly — glass, gasket, body and connection — never by the window alone. The <a href="/products/flanged-sight-glasses/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue hover:underline">SG-400 flanged sight glass</a> follows its flange class (ANSI B16.5 Class 150/300 or DIN PN10–PN40), and the <a href="/products/threaded-sight-glasses/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue hover:underline">SG-300 threaded sight glass</a> is rated to 100 bar (1450 psi). State operating and upset pressure: a sight glass that holds at steady state can still be challenged by cold-start transients.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="bg-slate-50">
        <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-blue">RECOMMENDED PRODUCTS</p>
          <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy-950 sm:text-3xl">SG-400 Flanged and SG-300 Threaded for Steam Duty</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Two models cover most steam and boiler applications. The SG-400 flanged sight glass is the primary choice for boiler drums, steam mains and heat exchanger covers, where operators need a real viewing window. The SG-300 threaded sight glass handles instrument lines, steam tracing manifolds and small-bore observation at pressures to 100 bar.</p>
          <div class="mt-8 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white shadow-sm">
            <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
              <thead class="bg-navy-950 text-white">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-5 py-4 font-semibold">Parameter</th>
                  <th class="px-5 py-4 font-semibold">SG-400 Flanged</th>
                  <th class="px-5 py-4 font-semibold">SG-300 Threaded</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">
                <tr><td class="px-5 py-4 font-bold text-navy-950">Mounting</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">ANSI B16.5 Class 150/300, DIN PN10–PN40</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">Male NPT / BSP, 1/4"–3"</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-5 py-4 font-bold text-navy-950">Glass</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">Borosilicate / soda lime / quartz</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">Borosilicate to DIN 7080</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-5 py-4 font-bold text-navy-950">Metal</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">Carbon steel / 316 SS</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">316 SS / Hastelloy / carbon steel</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-5 py-4 font-bold text-navy-950">Temperature</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">Borosilicate to 572°F (300°C); quartz beyond</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">Borosilicate to 572°F (300°C)</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-5 py-4 font-bold text-navy-950">Pressure</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">Per flange class (Class 150/300)</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">Up to 100 bar (1450 psi)</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-5 py-4 font-bold text-navy-950">Size range</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">2"–8" (DN50–DN200)</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">1/4"–3"</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-500">Both models are available in carbon steel bodies — the economical, field-proven choice for clean steam and condensate — with graphite-reinforced gaskets that hold their seal at flanged temperatures where elastomers soften. Where a bolted or threaded connection is impractical, the <a href="/products/weld-in-sight-glasses/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue hover:underline">weld-in sight glass</a> range offers a compact, permanent installation for small drums and manifolds.</p>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-500">Need to verify flow rather than just look inside? The <a href="/products/sight-flow-indicators/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue hover:underline">SG-200 sight flow indicator</a> adds flapper, rotary or drip-tube indication for condensate returns, steam trap discharge and valve position checks.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="bg-white">
        <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-blue">THERMAL SHOCK</p>
          <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy-950 sm:text-3xl">Glass Breakage Risk in Steam Service and How to Mitigate It</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">The glass rarely breaks because steam is hot — it breaks because steam is suddenly hot. Thermal shock is the stress created when one part of the window heats or cools faster than the rest: cold condensate splashing a hot glass, a cold glass hit by live steam during startup, or a rapid pressure change. Borosilicate's low thermal expansion resists this well; no glass resists it indefinitely.</p>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600"><strong class="text-navy-950">Operational mitigation.</strong> Drain condensate before opening steam to a cold sight glass, pressurize slowly during warm-up, and never quench a hot glass with cold water or cold feed. A controlled warm-up buys more service life than any material upgrade.</p>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600"><strong class="text-navy-950">Material mitigation.</strong> Specify the quartz window where temperatures exceed 572°F (300°C) or where thermal cycling is frequent — quartz carries heat and tolerates sharper gradients than borosilicate. Where debris or impact is a risk, fit a protective shield over the window. Graphite-reinforced gaskets keep the seal stable across the temperature swing, so the flanged joint does not leak as the assembly heats and cools.</p>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600"><strong class="text-navy-950">Boiler water level observation.</strong> On boiler drums and steam generators, the sight glass doubles as a water-level observation point — a direct visual check that confirms the level instruments. Keep the window clean: scale from hard feed water clouds the view over months of service, and a dirty glass hides the early chip that later becomes a leak path. The window is a consumable — inspect it for chips, cracks and etching at every shutdown, and keep a spare window and gasket per size from the <a href="/products/sight-glass-replacement-windows/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue hover:underline">replacement window range</a>.</p>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">More answers on glass choice and service life in the <a href="/faq/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue hover:underline">sight glass FAQ</a>, or send your steam pressure, temperature and connection to our engineers — see <a href="/contact/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue hover:underline">contact</a> for the fastest response.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </IndustryPageTemplate>

  <CtaBand
    title="Specifying a Sight Glass for Steam or Boiler Duty?"
    subtitle="Send your steam pressure, temperature and connection — we confirm the glass type (borosilicate or quartz), body and gasket combination within 24 hours."
  />
</Layout>
